<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Albert II of Germany</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1397">1397</a>–<a href="/years/1439">1439</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Albert V of Austria · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Albert II of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> was duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> from 1404 and, as son-in-law of Emperor Sigismund, became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> in 1437–1438 and king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 1438. He died in 1439 returning from a campaign against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>, and his posthumous son Ladislaus V was drawn into a long succession struggle in Hungary.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/albert-ii-of-germany.jpg" alt="Albert II of Germany" width="264" height="526" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Albrecht_II_von_Deutschland.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Albert was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> on 10 August 1397, the son of Duke Albert IV of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and Joanna Sophia of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He succeeded to the duchy at the age of seven on his father's death in 1404. His uncle Duke William of Inner Austria, of the rival Leopoldinian line, acted as regent, followed in 1406 by William's brothers Leopold IV and Ernest the Iron. Their quarrels and attempts to control the Albertinian territories produced near civil-war conditions. Albert, who had received a good education, took up the government of Austria proper in 1411, after Leopold's death, and with the help of his advisers cleared the duchy of the evils that had arisen during his minority.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In 1422 he married Elisabeth of Luxemburg, daughter and heiress of King Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>, who later became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> and king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>. The marriage brought Albert claims to many realms of Central and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>. He assisted Sigismund against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussites">Hussites</a>, which drew the Austrian duchy into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hussite-wars">Hussite Wars</a>; Sigismund designated him his successor and made him Margrave of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a> in 1423. The Austrian lands were devastated several times, and Albert took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-domazlice">Battle of Domažlice</a> in 1431, where the imperial troops were defeated.</p><p>When Sigismund died in 1437, Albert was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> on 1 January 1438 and moved his court there. He was crowned king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a> six months later but could not take possession of the country, and he was fighting the Bohemians and their Polish allies when he was elected King of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> on 17 March 1438. He was never crowned emperor. His wars against heretics went with persecution of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. Taxes were laid on Jewish communities to fund his campaigns, and after Hussite raids Austrian Jews were accused of collaboration and arms trading. A charge of host desecration at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/enns-river">Enns</a> in 1420 gave him a pretext to destroy the community.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the chronicler Thomas Ebendorfer, on 23 May 1420 Albert ordered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> imprisoned and forcibly converted; those who would not convert were sent off in boats down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, and wealthy Jews were held, some tortured and stripped of property. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-martin-v">Pope Martin V</a> intervened to stop the forced baptism of children. On 12 March 1421 Albert sentenced the remaining Jews to death, and ninety-two men and 120 women were burned at the stake outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>'s walls. He died at Neszmély on 27 October 1439 while defending <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> against the Turks, and was buried at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/szekesfehervar">Székesfehérvár</a>. His children included Ladislas V Posthumus, who was born after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Albert II of Germany” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ladislaus the Posthumous, son of <b>Albert II of Germany</b>, was crowned king of Bohemia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-28">28 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in Neszmély, in what is now <a href="/countries/hungary">Hungary</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1424" aria-label="Vienna on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4931 477 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Albert II of Germany"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5381 747h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5381 747h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.21,16.37&amp;year=1424">What was here in 1424? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
